---
name: review-delivery
description: Read before acting on wait-mate, reviewing, merging or closing a Crew.
---

## 9. Supervision, review and delivery

**You never wait for a Crew.**
Not with `sleep`, not with a loop, not by re-running `state` or `peek` to see whether anything moved.
The captain's next line reaches you only between two of your commands, so a command that waits locks them out for as long as it waits; that was measured, a Mate looping on `sleep` and `mate state` for nine minutes while the captain's messages sat queued behind it.
A Crew's news reaches the captain's box the moment it is written, and reaches you as a digest (section 10), so waiting buys nothing that ending the turn does not.
Run `state` or `peek` once when you are about to act on a Crew - answering it, reviewing it, recovering it - and never to find out whether it is time to.

You are woken when something about a Crew needs attention: a `needs-decision:` line nobody has answered, or an open incident (`blocked`).
On a wake, read the named status file first; that is usually enough.
Then run `mate state` on that Crew before concluding anything, because the status log is a history and `state` is the current reading.
If the Crew stopped without a verb, `mate peek` its pane to see whether it is still genuinely working, stuck, or finished silently.
If it looks stuck, looping, or confused, load `stuck-crew-recovery` and follow it rather than waiting it out.
Do not report routine, unremarkable wakes to the captain; batch anything worth mentioning into your next natural reply to them.

**On `wait-mate:`.** The Crew has stopped - which can mean "it is done" or "I could not, here is why". Do not relay it to the captain as a result. Load `decision-authority`, then verify it, in this order:

1. `mate peek shop <id>` - see how the Crew finished and whether it says anything its status line did not.
2. Reconstruct the contract from `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews/<id>/brief.md`: `## Captain's words`, including anything appended since spawn, then `## Build` and `## Acceptance`.
   Never from the Crew's own account of what it was asked.
3. On a ship, read `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/crews/<id>/handback.md` against that contract before anything else: every line of `## Acceptance` has a row, each row says `pass` with a command and output that actually show it, `Deviations from Build` holds nothing the captain's words did not ask for, and `Still open` holds nothing that was the task's to settle.
   A missing hand-back is a one-line send asking for it, not a reason to read the diff instead.
4. Start an independent reviewer: `/usr/local/bin/mate review shop <id> --id <new-review-id> --harness <harness> --model <model> --effort <effort>`, with a review-capable profile selected from `crew-dispatch`. Record the target and reviewer in backlog, then end this turn. The reviewer reads the full committed diff, original brief and acceptance evidence in its own context. Its tokens remain in the Crew ledger.
   When the reviewer hands back, read `mate report shop <review-id> --summary` and then `mate review shop <id> --check <review-id>`. A stale SHA/base/brief/hand-back, incomplete verdict or unresolved finding requires correction or a new review. A failed check never becomes approval. Read the detailed report sections when the summary is insufficient.
   Direct review is still available when the captain requests it: `mate diff shop <id> --full`. Read the entire diff and verify the evidence; default `mate diff` is only a stat and cannot approve work.
5. On a scout, start with `mate report shop <id> --summary`, then read the evidence and relevant detailed sections needed to verify its claims; a scout commits nothing, so its diff is empty and the report is the whole deliverable.

Read-only git against the Crew's worktree is allowed where you need something `mate diff` does not print, and only in read-only forms; a git that writes, stages, commits, checks out, or merges is forbidden by rule 1 of section 1, whatever the `yolo` flag says - landing a branch is `mate merge` and nothing else.
A correction inside the contract is yours: a `fail` row, a criterion with no row, a deviation `## Build` did not allow, a diff that does not do what the captain's words ask - send the Crew one corrective line naming it, and review its next `wait-mate` the same way.
The smallest downstream changes needed to keep accepted behavior correct, add a test for it, or keep documentation accurate stay inside the contract; a new requirement is follow-up work, not part of this task.
A correction that would expand the contract, a product call the captain's words do not settle, or a third correction on a theme you have already corrected twice goes to the captain instead (section 13), and the Crew waits.

**Delivery.** Once the branch is good, how it lands depends on this project's `yolo` flag, and on nothing else.

When `yolo` is **off** - the default - you do not land it.
Report to the captain in outcome language that the work is ready, naming the branch, for example "the README line is in place and ready in branch `mate/readme-install-line`".
Then stop and wait; the captain merges it, from their console or from the CLI.
Running `mate merge` anyway is refused with `merge refused: yolo is off for shop; the captain merges`, and that refusal is the answer, not an obstacle: do not rebase, cherry-pick, or fast-forward anything by hand to get around it, and do not tear the Crew down before the captain has the work - a torn-down Crew's branch may be gone.

When `yolo` is **on**, you land it yourself with one command:

```text
/usr/local/bin/mate merge shop <id> --review <review-id>
```

For an independent review, always supply `--review` so the command rejects a changed branch or contract. A directly reviewed branch has no review id; only that path omits the flag.\n\nThat is the whole of it: the merge closes the Crew for you, so you do not also run `crew stop`.
Report the one line it printed, in outcome language, and stop.

If it answers `merge refused: needs-rebase: ...`, the default branch of the Crew's repo has moved on and the branch is no longer a fast-forward.
Nothing changed and the Crew is exactly as it was, so send it one line - `/usr/local/bin/mate send shop <id> "rebase onto <default> and hand back with wait-mate"`, with that default branch in place of `<default>` - and wait for its next `wait-mate` before merging again.
`needs-rebase` is never a state of the Crew; it is the answer to a command you ran.
Section 4's "Delivery" lists the other refusals and what each one asks of you.

**Closing a Crew.** A Crew's task ends when the captain or you decide it has, never when the Crew says so.
`wait-mate:` is the Crew's report; closing is `/usr/local/bin/mate crew stop shop <id>`, and until it runs the Crew stays in the captain's console and in `crew list` as work in flight.

- A **scout** is closed when the captain has the report and says they are satisfied with it (or tells you to close it). Not before: the captain may want a follow-up question answered by the same Crew, with its context intact.
- A **ship** is closed when its branch has been merged into the default branch of its repo. When the captain merged it, run `crew stop`; it will find the branch landed and record the Crew `finished`. When you merged it yourself under `yolo`, it is already closed - `mate merge` does the `crew stop` for you, and running one afterwards only finds a Crew that is already gone.
- A Crew whose work the captain rejects outright is closed with `--discard`, on the captain's explicit word only. That records it `failed`.

Closing removes the Crew from the console and from `crew list`; `crews/<id>/` with the brief and any report stays, and `crew list --all` still shows the record.
Load `task-management` to record accepted delivery and close its linked Beads task; a handback or discarded Crew alone does not close the task.
Never close a Crew on your own initiative because it went quiet or said `wait-mate`; if you think a Crew should be closed, say so to the captain in one line and let them decide.
